  var BUS5_QUESTION_BANK = [
    { q: 'What are economies of scale?', options: ['The cost advantages a business gains as it increases its scale of production, lowering average costs per unit', 'A rise in average costs as a business grows larger', 'A type of promotional pricing strategy', 'The total revenue earned by a business in a year'], correct: 0, explain: 'Economies of scale are the cost savings a business achieves as output increases, spreading fixed costs over more units and often gaining bulk-buying discounts, lowering average cost per unit.', misconception: 'Rising average costs as a business grows too large describes diseconomies of scale, the opposite effect.', tag: 'Economies of scale' },
    { q: 'Which of these is an example of an internal economy of scale?', options: ['Bulk-buying raw materials at a discount because of a larger order size', 'A new motorway being built near the business by the government', 'A general fall in national interest rates', 'A change in exchange rates'], correct: 0, explain: 'Bulk-buying discounts are an internal economy of scale, arising directly from the business’s own growth in purchasing power.', misconception: 'A new motorway, interest rate changes and exchange rate changes are external factors outside the business’s control, not internal economies.', tag: 'Economies of scale' },
    { q: 'What is an external economy of scale?', options: ['A cost advantage gained because of the growth of the whole industry or area, not just one business', 'A cost advantage gained only through a business’s own bulk buying', 'A type of diseconomy caused by a business becoming too large', 'A pricing strategy used when launching a new product'], correct: 0, explain: 'External economies of scale arise from factors outside an individual business, such as improved local infrastructure or a skilled local workforce, benefiting all businesses in that industry or area.', misconception: 'Economies arising purely from one business’s own bulk-buying power are internal, not external, economies of scale.', tag: 'Economies of scale' },
    { q: 'What are diseconomies of scale?', options: ['Rising average costs per unit that can occur when a business grows too large', 'Falling average costs per unit as output increases', 'A method of quality control', 'A source of business finance'], correct: 0, explain: 'Diseconomies of scale occur when a business becomes too large to manage efficiently, causing average costs per unit to rise, for example due to poor communication or coordination problems.', misconception: 'Falling average costs as output increases describes economies of scale, the opposite of diseconomies.', tag: 'Economies of scale' },
    { q: 'Why might a business experience diseconomies of scale as it grows very large?', options: ['Communication and coordination can become more difficult, leading to inefficiency', 'It will always benefit from unlimited bulk-buying discounts', 'Its fixed costs are automatically eliminated', 'It becomes legally required to reduce output'], correct: 0, explain: 'As a business grows very large, it can become harder to manage and communicate effectively across the whole organisation, leading to inefficiencies and rising average costs.', misconception: 'Bulk-buying discounts do not increase without limit, and there is no legal requirement forcing a large business to cut output.', tag: 'Economies of scale' },
    { q: 'What best describes job production?', options: ['Producing a single, often unique, item to a customer’s specific requirements', 'Producing large volumes of an identical, standardised product continuously', 'Producing items in identifiable groups or batches', 'Producing goods using no labour at all'], correct: 0, explain: 'Job production involves making one unique product at a time, often tailored to a specific customer, such as a bespoke wedding cake or a custom-built ship.', misconception: 'Producing large volumes of an identical product continuously describes flow production, not job production.', tag: 'Production processes' },
    { q: 'What is a key characteristic of batch production?', options: ['Producing goods in identifiable groups, with each batch potentially varying slightly from the last', 'Producing only one unique item at a time for a single customer', 'Producing an unlimited, continuous stream of an identical product with no breaks', 'Producing goods without following any set process'], correct: 0, explain: 'Batch production involves making a set quantity (a batch) of a product before switching the process to make a different batch, allowing some variation between batches.', misconception: 'Continuous, identical output with no breaks describes flow production, while single unique items describe job production, not batch.', tag: 'Production processes' },
    { q: 'What is a key characteristic of flow production?', options: ['A continuous, standardised production process, often using an assembly line, making large volumes of identical products', 'Making a single, unique product to order', 'Making small groups of similar products at a time', 'Producing goods only when a specific customer places an order'], correct: 0, explain: 'Flow production involves a continuous process, often on an assembly line, producing large volumes of a standardised, identical product efficiently.', misconception: 'Producing to order for a single customer describes job production, distinct from the continuous, mass nature of flow production.', tag: 'Production processes' },
    { q: 'What is a key advantage of flow production?', options: ['It can achieve low unit costs through economies of scale when producing large volumes', 'It allows every product to be fully customised for each customer', 'It requires very little upfront investment in equipment', 'It is the most flexible production method when demand changes frequently'], correct: 0, explain: 'Flow production can achieve low costs per unit due to the large volumes produced, benefiting from economies of scale, though it requires significant investment and is relatively inflexible.', misconception: 'Flow production is generally less flexible and requires high upfront investment, and it does not offer full product customisation.', tag: 'Production processes' },
    { q: 'What is the difference between labour-intensive and capital-intensive production?', options: ['Labour-intensive production relies mainly on workers; capital-intensive production relies mainly on machinery and equipment', 'Labour-intensive production always costs more than capital-intensive production', 'Capital-intensive production requires no initial investment', 'There is no meaningful difference between the two'], correct: 0, explain: 'Labour-intensive production depends primarily on human workers to carry out tasks, while capital-intensive production relies mainly on machinery, technology and equipment.', misconception: 'Capital-intensive production usually requires significant upfront investment in machinery, contrary to requiring none.', tag: 'Production processes' },
    { q: 'A factory produces 4,800 units in a week using 40 workers, each working 40 hours. What is labour productivity, in units per worker?', options: ['120 units per worker', '4,800 units per worker', '40 units per worker', '192,000 units per worker'], correct: 0, explain: 'Labour productivity (units per worker) = total output ÷ number of workers = 4,800 ÷ 40 = 120 units per worker.', misconception: 'A common error is dividing by total hours worked instead of the number of workers, or forgetting to divide at all.', tag: 'Production processes' },
    { q: 'How can improvements in productivity benefit a business?', options: ['They can lower the cost of producing each unit, improving competitiveness and potential profit', 'They always require a business to reduce output', 'They guarantee an increase in the number of employees needed', 'They have no effect on a business’s costs'], correct: 0, explain: 'Higher productivity means more output is produced for the same input (such as labour or time), which can lower unit costs and improve a business’s competitiveness and profitability.', misconception: 'Improved productivity does not require reducing output — quite the opposite, it usually means producing the same or more with the same or fewer resources.', tag: 'Production processes' },
    { q: 'What is a core principle of lean production?', options: ['Minimising waste in all its forms to reduce costs and improve efficiency', 'Producing as much stock as possible in advance, regardless of demand', 'Ignoring product quality in favour of speed', 'Avoiding any form of continuous improvement'], correct: 0, explain: 'Lean production focuses on eliminating waste — of time, materials, or effort — throughout the production process, improving efficiency and reducing costs.', misconception: 'Lean production actually aims to reduce excess stock and waste, and it typically goes hand-in-hand with maintaining or improving quality.', tag: 'Lean production' },
    { q: 'How does just-in-time (JIT) production operate?', options: ['Materials and components are ordered and received only as they are needed for production, minimising stock held', 'Large amounts of stock are ordered well in advance and stored for months', 'Products are made without following any customer orders', 'All raw materials are purchased once a year in a single large order'], correct: 0, explain: 'JIT production involves ordering and receiving materials only when needed for production, reducing the amount of stock a business needs to hold and store.', misconception: 'Storing large amounts of stock in advance is the opposite of JIT, which is often contrasted with traditional stockpiling approaches.', tag: 'Lean production' },
    { q: 'What is a key risk of using just-in-time (JIT) production?', options: ['A disruption to the supply chain can quickly halt production, since little or no buffer stock is held', 'It always increases the amount of stock a business must store', 'It guarantees a business will never run out of materials', 'It removes the need for reliable suppliers'], correct: 0, explain: 'Because JIT relies on minimal stock levels, any delay or disruption in the supply chain can quickly bring production to a halt, since there is little buffer stock to fall back on.', misconception: 'JIT reduces stored stock rather than increasing it, and it actually depends heavily on having reliable, well-coordinated suppliers.', tag: 'Lean production' },
    { q: 'What is the principle behind Kaizen (continuous improvement)?', options: ['Making small, ongoing improvements to processes, often involving employees at all levels', 'Making one single, very large change to production once every ten years', 'Focusing only on senior management decisions, without employee input', 'Avoiding any changes to existing working methods'], correct: 0, explain: 'Kaizen involves making small, continuous improvements to processes over time, often drawing on the ideas and involvement of employees at all levels of the business.', misconception: 'Kaizen is about frequent, incremental change involving staff input, not a single large change or a management-only approach.', tag: 'Lean production' },
    { q: 'Why is using resources effectively important to a business?', options: ['It can reduce costs and waste, improving efficiency and competitiveness', 'It has no effect on a business’s profitability', 'It only matters for businesses in the manufacturing sector', 'It always requires a business to increase its workforce'], correct: 0, explain: 'Using resources — such as materials, time and labour — effectively helps reduce waste and cost, supporting greater efficiency and competitiveness across any type of business.', misconception: 'Effective resource use matters across all sectors, not just manufacturing, and it is about efficiency, not necessarily hiring more staff.', tag: 'Lean production' },
    { q: 'How can robotics be used in production?', options: ['To automate repetitive tasks, improving speed, consistency and precision', 'Only to complete tasks that require creative human judgement', 'To completely replace the need for any product design', 'Only in the service sector, never in manufacturing'], correct: 0, explain: 'Robotics can automate repetitive or precise manufacturing tasks, improving consistency, speed and accuracy in the production process.', misconception: 'Robotics is most suited to repetitive, well-defined tasks rather than tasks needing creative judgement, and it is widely used in manufacturing.', tag: 'Technology in production' },
    { q: 'What is a limitation of using robotics in production?', options: ['High upfront investment costs, and the potential need to retrain or reduce the workforce', 'Robots always produce lower-quality output than human workers', 'Robots cannot be used in any factory setting', 'Robotics eliminates the need for any maintenance'], correct: 0, explain: 'Introducing robotics can require significant initial investment, and may lead to job losses or a need to retrain staff to work alongside new technology.', misconception: 'Robotics is often used specifically to improve consistency and precision, so it does not generally lower output quality compared with manual production.', tag: 'Technology in production' },
    { q: 'How can new production technology increase flexibility for a business?', options: ['It can allow production to be adjusted or reprogrammed more easily to make different products or variations', 'It always makes a business less able to respond to changes in demand', 'It removes the need for any human oversight', 'It only benefits businesses using flow production'], correct: 0, explain: 'Modern production technology can often be reprogrammed or adjusted relatively easily, allowing a business to switch between products or respond to changing customer demand.', misconception: 'New technology tends to improve, not reduce, a business’s ability to respond to changing demand, and human oversight is still typically needed.', tag: 'Technology in production' },
    { q: 'What should a business consider when assessing the impact of introducing new production technology?', options: ['The effect on costs, quality, employees and the need for staff retraining', 'Only the initial purchase price of the technology', 'Only how the technology looks compared with competitors’ equipment', 'Nothing — new technology always benefits a business in every way'], correct: 0, explain: 'Assessing new technology involves weighing up its effect on costs, product quality, and its impact on employees, including potential job losses or the need for retraining.', misconception: 'New technology brings both potential benefits and risks — it does not automatically benefit a business in every respect.', tag: 'Technology in production' },
    { q: 'Which of these is one of the four factors of production?', options: ['Land', 'Advertising', 'Market share', 'Break-even'], correct: 0, explain: 'The four factors of production are enterprise, capital, land and labour — the resources needed to produce goods and services.', misconception: 'Advertising, market share and break-even are marketing or finance concepts, not among the four factors of production.', tag: 'Factors of production' },
    { q: 'What is the role of “enterprise” as a factor of production?', options: ['Taking the risk of organising the other factors of production to create goods or services', 'Providing the raw materials used in production', 'Providing the physical effort of workers', 'Providing the money invested in machinery'], correct: 0, explain: 'Enterprise refers to the entrepreneurial skill of organising the other factors of production — land, labour and capital — and taking on the associated business risk.', misconception: 'Providing raw materials relates to land, providing physical effort relates to labour, and providing machinery relates to capital, not enterprise.', tag: 'Factors of production' },
    { q: 'What does “capital” refer to as a factor of production?', options: ['Man-made resources used in production, such as machinery, tools and buildings', 'Natural resources such as raw materials and land itself', 'The workforce employed by a business', 'The entrepreneurial risk-taking involved in starting a business'], correct: 0, explain: 'Capital, as a factor of production, refers to man-made resources such as machinery, equipment, tools and buildings used to produce goods and services.', misconception: 'Natural resources are the “land” factor of production, and the workforce is “labour” — distinct from capital.', tag: 'Factors of production' },
    { q: 'What does “land” refer to as a factor of production?', options: ['Natural resources, including the land itself and raw materials it provides', 'The buildings and machinery a business owns', 'The workers employed to carry out tasks', 'The money used to fund business operations'], correct: 0, explain: 'Land, as a factor of production, covers natural resources, including the physical land itself and raw materials such as minerals, timber and water.', misconception: 'Buildings and machinery are capital, and workers are labour — both distinct from the natural-resource factor of land.', tag: 'Factors of production' },
    { q: 'Why might a business choose a labour-intensive method of production?', options: ['Where tasks require flexibility, human judgement, or where labour is relatively cheap compared with machinery', 'Because labour-intensive methods always produce output faster than machines', 'Because it removes the need to pay any wages', 'Because labour-intensive production always has lower total costs than capital-intensive production'], correct: 0, explain: 'A business may choose labour-intensive production where flexibility or human skill and judgement are valuable, or where labour costs are relatively low compared with investing in machinery.', misconception: 'Labour-intensive production still requires paying wages, and it is not automatically faster or cheaper than capital-intensive methods — it depends on the specific business context.', tag: 'Factors of production' },
    { q: 'What does “quality” mean in relation to goods and services?', options: ['Meeting or exceeding customer expectations in terms of fitness for purpose, reliability and standard', 'Producing the cheapest possible version of a product', 'Producing the largest possible quantity of a product', 'Using the newest available production technology, regardless of the product'], correct: 0, explain: 'Quality refers to how well a good or service meets customer expectations and its intended purpose, including factors such as reliability, durability and standard of finish.', misconception: 'Quality is about meeting customer expectations, not simply about being the cheapest, the most abundant, or made with the newest technology.', tag: 'Quality' },
    { q: 'How can high quality benefit a business?', options: ['It can improve customer satisfaction, build reputation, and provide a competitive advantage', 'It always increases production costs with no other benefit', 'It removes the need for any quality checks in future', 'It has no impact on customer loyalty'], correct: 0, explain: 'High-quality goods and services can boost customer satisfaction and loyalty, strengthen a business’s reputation, and help it stand out from competitors.', misconception: 'While maintaining quality can involve costs, it typically brings offsetting benefits like stronger reputation and customer loyalty, not cost with no return.', tag: 'Quality' },
    { q: 'What is the main principle of quality control?', options: ['Checking a product for faults, usually at the end of the production process, before it reaches the customer', 'Building quality checks into every single stage carried out by every employee', 'Ignoring product quality until a customer complains', 'Reducing production speed to zero to guarantee no faults'], correct: 0, explain: 'Quality control typically involves inspecting products, often at the end of production, to identify and remove faulty items before they reach customers.', misconception: 'Involving every employee in checking quality at every stage more closely describes Total Quality Management (TQM), rather than traditional quality control.', tag: 'Quality' },
    { q: 'What is a limitation of quality control compared with Total Quality Management (TQM)?', options: ['Checking only at the end of production can mean faults and wasted resources have already occurred earlier in the process', 'Quality control involves every employee in checking quality throughout production', 'Quality control guarantees zero product defects', 'Quality control is always more expensive to implement than TQM'], correct: 0, explain: 'Because quality control often checks products only at the end of the process, faults may have already led to wasted materials and time earlier in production — a key limitation compared with TQM’s continuous approach.', misconception: 'It is TQM, not traditional quality control, that typically involves all employees checking quality throughout the whole production process.', tag: 'Quality' },
    { q: 'What is the core idea behind Total Quality Management (TQM)?', options: ['Every employee, at every stage, takes responsibility for maintaining and improving quality', 'Only a single quality inspector checks products at the very end of production', 'Quality is only the responsibility of senior management', 'Quality checks should only happen once a year'], correct: 0, explain: 'TQM is an approach where quality is everyone’s responsibility, built into every stage of production, aiming to prevent faults rather than just detect them at the end.', misconception: 'A single end-of-line inspector describes basic quality control, not the whole-business, prevention-focused approach of TQM.', tag: 'Quality' }
  ];

